Part 2:

Ok, so I’ve briefly explained how the process works in five steps
If you need a reminder or have no idea what I’m talking about, here are those steps again:
Step 1: I do my Research using any social links
Step 2: I brainstorm and come up with an idea for a post
Step 3: I write until it makes sense (first draft usually does)
Step 4: I post the file 
Step 5: Done!
But if you wAnt a more in-depth overview, then I’d be happy to oblige and tell y’all about it

Alright, so first things first, what “social links” am I talking about here?
Well, on this specific repo, I have a wiki page known as “Important Links” where I post everything online that you need in order to become a FNF superfan
Some of those links I frequently visit (what, you think I put them there for show) and new information is found

After finding the information I need, I do a bit of brainstorming
This usually includes contemplating for a few minuteS and then writing what comes to mind
Yeah, that’s about it for Step 2, which takes all of 1-5 minutes to accomplish
Coming up for a topic for a blog post is surprisingly easy (not to jinx it), especially if there is some new breaking development going on in the social links

Now that I’ve thought up what I want to write, then it’s actually time to start writing
I treat these blog posts like a free-flow piece, just writing what the mind thinks of at the moment and sticking with it
Almost always, the first draft ends being the final product that I “upload” to the Github repo
I do aLl my writing on a separate document and then I copy over the contents over to a new file created in “the_posts” branch
This is because I like the formatting of my specific program I’m using to write this (no, it isn’t Notepad, lol) 
and actually prefer it over Github cluttered writing style
It’s also because Github’s style does not indicate how long a sentence can be, but my program of choice does, 
so that way, y’all don’t have to scroll horizontally to see the end of a line
I try to keep each line short but sometimes I feel the need to fit extra information, so bare that in mind

After writing, as I’ve said previously, I copy all the contents over to another file and I post it
Voila, that’s basically the entire writing process in 4 steps
There is a fifth step that I occasionally do, which involves checking for grammar, modifying files, and potentiAlly censoring unwanted information
(and yes, this step was done when I deleted the crashout I had in “Let’s be real for a second here”, but it’s also been done to many others, 
let’s not dwell too hard on it, okay)
